13
00:00:50,480 --> 00:00:53,760
I actually have been working with these 3D technologies for a while.

14
00:00:53,760 --> 00:00:58,560
I worked on them with uh NASA for a while, and it's always been interest of mine.

15
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:03,760
The easiest system that people can approach right now are L C D shutter glasses.

16
00:01:03,920 --> 00:01:20,080
These are glasses, these are electronic glasses that work either with a uh monitor, a CRT monitor that's capable of high refresh rates, or with what some of the newer projectors that can support refresh rates of about a hundred hertz or a hundred and twenty hertz.

17
00:01:20,080 --> 00:01:27,440
The basic principle is that the monitor displays left and right eye images in succession.

18
00:01:27,440 --> 00:01:30,240
It's called frame sequential uh stereo.

19
00:01:30,240 --> 00:01:39,360
Um and the glasses alternately block out the right and left eye, thus giving the user the illusion of 3D inside the monitor.

20
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:44,560
Uh the problem, the main problem with these is one, you need a graphics card capable of driving them.

21
00:01:44,560 --> 00:01:49,040
And two, if your refresh rate is not high enough, it gives you a headache.

22
00:01:49,040 --> 00:01:51,200
You can see the flicker from fluorescent lights.

23
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:57,120
However, most 3D technologies right now are based on this or something similar to it.

24
00:01:57,120 --> 00:02:01,160
Uh and you know, I'm looking to resell some of these technologies, so I shouldn't bash them too much.

25
00:02:02,440 --> 00:02:08,760
But e Dimensional is a company that makes the cheaper version of these glasses.

26
00:02:08,760 --> 00:02:13,480
Uh crystallize on the other, I mean that's about a hundred, two hundred dollars per pair.

27
00:02:13,480 --> 00:02:22,120
On the other side of the spectrum is crystallized, which you know, places like NASA use which have the better quality.

28
00:02:22,120 --> 00:02:27,640
This is, you know, starting at the low end, that at the higher end you have similar systems.

29
00:02:27,640 --> 00:02:31,800
You have dual projector stereographic vision systems.

30
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:35,320
Places like IMAX and some other movie theaters use this.

31
00:02:35,320 --> 00:02:46,840
This is where you take two projectors, either film or electronic, you stack them together with polarizing filters on each of them, and shoot the images at a non-depolarizing screen.

32
00:02:46,840 --> 00:02:52,360
So it has to be a special kind of a screen, um like a silver screen that's not necessarily cheap.

33
00:02:52,760 --> 00:02:55,960
Um but a lot of movie theaters might be equipped with them.

34
00:02:55,960 --> 00:03:08,280
Um you wear what are called passive polarized uh glasses, where each um each side of the glasses blocks a part of the image.

35
00:03:08,280 --> 00:03:10,440
Thus you get an illusion of stereo.

